data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span` of native-sized integers.** Both `nint` and `nuint` work:

```csharp
Span<nint> span = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> uspan = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

As the test confirmed, these produce `Span<IntPtr>` and `Span<UIntPtr>` respectively, and behave exactly as expected. This has been supported since C# 10 / .NET 6 (when `nint`/`nuint` became first-class types backed by `IntPtr`/`UIntPtr`).
